data.content:
## What happened

In C# 14 / .NET 10, new **span overload resolution rules** cause `array.Contains(num)` inside an expression tree to bind to `MemoryExtensions.Contains(ReadOnlySpan<T>, T)` instead of `Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)`.

Expression trees **cannot represent calls to span-based methods** (spans are ref structs), so this throws at runtime when the expression is compiled/interpreted.

## How to fix

Force the compiler to resolve `Enumerable.Contains` by casting the array to `IEnumerable<T>`:

```csharp
// Option 1: Cast
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);

// Option 2: .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 3: Explicit static call
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

Any of these prevent the span overload from being selected and restore the .NET 9 behavior.
